ARM: emulate ARMv6K exclusive loads and stores for ARMv6 user space

The ARM1136 r0 (OMAP2420) is plain ARMv6: LDREX and STREX for words
only. ARMv6K added the byte, halfword and doubleword forms and CLREX,
and code built for ARMv6 uses them all the same: LLVM emits them for 8-,
16- and 64-bit atomics even for -mcpu=arm1136j-s, so Rust's prebuilt std
for arm-unknown-linux-gnueabi has some 100 LDREXD and a dozen LDREXB,
and any Rust program dies with SIGILL on its first such atomic.

Emulate them for user space in ARM state with an undefined instruction
hook, as SWP emulation does on ARMv7. The exclusive monitor is per task:
the address, the size and the task's context switch count at the LDREX.
A STREX stores only if the monitor matches and the task hasn't been
switched out since, which on a uniprocessor means nothing else wrote
there; the load with its monitor and the check with its store each run
with preemption and page faults off. Signal delivery, fork, exec, CLREX
and every STREX clear the monitor. Misaligned accesses raise SIGBUS,
the UNPREDICTABLE register choices SIGILL. The ARMv6K hints (YIELD,
WFE, WFI, SEV) are NOPs should they trap. Counts in
/proc/cpu/v6k_emulation; a rate-limited note per task on first use.
Thumb-1, the only Thumb on this core, has no exclusives.

